// the element loop that made `array<string>` correct must not have cost `array<int32>` its bulk copy.
//
// `array<T>`'s copy constructor branches on `mem::is_trivially_copyable<T>()`, and both arms are correct
// for every `T` - the branch exists only to buy back the `memcpy`. That makes the fast path exactly the
// kind of thing that regresses without anything failing: an `array<int32>` copied element by element
// still produces the right answer, just one store at a time, and no OUT section anywhere would notice.
//
// So this pins the lowering rather than the result - and what it pins is now an *absence*. The branch is a
// `const if`, so AST::ConstFolding replaces the statement with the arm the predicate selected before codegen
// ever sees it: what reaches the IR is a `memcpy`, and the element loop is not there at all rather than
// there and unreachable.

array<int32> $a = [1, 2, 3];
array<int32> $b = $a;

$b[0] = 9;

// distinct buffers, so the copy really happened
echo $a[0];
echo $b[0];
echo $b->count();
